    {
      "id": 5,
      "slug": "knowledge-check",
      "title": "Knowledge Check",
      "description_html": "<p>Before you complete this program, apply everything you have learned across all five modules in the final scenario based assessment.&nbsp;This final assessment will test your ability to apply optical switching concepts to real world data center scenarios.</p><p></p><p>Select \"Start Quiz\" to begin.</p>",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "type": "knowledge_check",
          "question_type": "multiple_choice",
          "question_html": "<p>Your team is designing the network fabric for a massive new<strong> language model training cluster.</strong> The architecture requires an extremely high port count to scale out <strong>thousands of GPUs</strong>.</p><p></p><p>The workload phases are <strong>highly predictable</strong>, meaning the network only needs to reconfigure every few minutes. Which optical hardware approach provides the <strong>best physical fit</strong> for this specific cluster?</p>",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text_html": "<p>Traditional electrical packet switches, because they offer the only reliable way to buffer traffic during long training phases.</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>Silicon Photonics, because its nanosecond switching speeds will eliminate all latency during the training run.</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>3D MEMS, because it provides the massive port density required for high radix scaling and the slower mechanical switching time is acceptable for predictable workloads.</p>",
              "correct": true
            }
          ],
          "feedback_correct_html": "<p>Correct. 3D MEMS provides the high port density needed for massive clusters. Since the workload reconfigures infrequently, the slower millisecond actuation time of physical mirrors is not a bottleneck.</p>",
          "feedback_incorrect_html": "<p>Incorrect. While silicon photonics is incredibly fast, it currently lacks the high port density needed for this massive scale. MEMS is the correct choice because it provides high radix, and the slower switching time is perfectly acceptable for predictable workload phases.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "type": "knowledge_check",
          "question_type": "multiple_choice",
          "question_html": "<p>A facility manager is planning a massive hardware refresh to upgrade a cluster's leaf switches <strong>from 400G to 800G transceivers</strong>. The cluster currently uses a <strong>flat OCS spine layer</strong>.</p><p></p><p>What specific <strong>physical upgrades</strong> must the engineering team perform on the core OCS spine hardware to support this doubled data rate?</p>",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text_html": "<p>The internal optical-electrical-optical (OEO) converters within the spine must be swapped out for 800G compatible modules.</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>The OCS spine requires zero physical upgrades because it simply reflects pure light and operates completely independent of the underlying data rate.</p>",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>The physical MEMS mirrors must be recalibrated to reflect higher frequency wavelengths.</p>",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "feedback_correct_html": "<p>Correct. This is the primary benefit of a rate-agnostic optical spine. It simply bounces light, meaning you can upgrade the edge transceivers indefinitely without touching the core routing hardware.</p>",
          "feedback_incorrect_html": "<p>Incorrect. An OCS spine has no optical-electrical-optical converters and does not care about data rates. Because it simply reflects light, it requires zero physical upgrades to support faster transceivers at the edge.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "type": "knowledge_check",
          "question_type": "multiple_choice",
          "question_html": "<p>During an active training run on a programmable Superpod, a <strong>catastrophic thermal event</strong> destroys an entire rack of compute nodes.</p><p></p><p>To prevent the entire distributed training job from failing, how does the <strong>optical control plane</strong> resolve the<strong> physical disruption</strong>?</p>",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text_html": "<p>It dynamically re-angles the physical mirrors within the optical switches to bypass the dead rack and weave the remaining healthy cubes back into a continuous torus topology.</p>",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>It instantly converts all optical signals back to electrical packets and routes them through a secondary backup spine.</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>It relies on deep packet buffers at the leaf layer to hold the data until a technician physically replaces the melted fibers.</p>",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "feedback_correct_html": "<p>Correct. The physical layer is programmable. The software control plane immediately manipulates the hardware mirrors to route light around the failure, restoring full bandwidth to the remaining healthy nodes.</p>",
          "feedback_incorrect_html": "<p>Incorrect. The optical fabric does not fall back on electrical conversions or buffers. It uses the physical mirrors to dynamically redirect light around the failed hardware, closing the topological gap instantly.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "type": "knowledge_check",
          "question_type": "multiple_choice",
          "question_html": "<p>A research team is deploying a Mixture-of-Experts (<strong>MoE</strong>) model that relies heavily on <strong>Expert Parallelism.</strong> This specific strategy generates<strong> intense, highly localized, and unpredictable bursts</strong> of communication between <strong>specific groups of servers</strong>. </p><p></p><p>Which <strong>network topology </strong>strategy best prevents these bursts from congesting the entire data center fabric?</p>",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text_html": "<p>Forcing all all-to-all communication through the localized intra-server electrical mesh.</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>Isolating the Expert Parallelism traffic onto a dedicated, dynamically reconfigurable regional optical layer like MixNet.</p>",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>Funneling all traffic through a centralized, static electrical spine to ensure deep packet inspection.</p>",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "feedback_correct_html": "<p>Correct. Architectures like MixNet are specifically designed to offload intense, unpredictable traffic bursts onto a dedicated regional optical layer, leaving the global electrical network free for standard communication.</p>",
          "feedback_incorrect_html": "<p>Incorrect. MoE traffic will easily overwhelm a local electrical mesh or a static centralized spine. The correct approach is isolating that specific traffic onto a dynamically reconfigurable regional optical layer.</p>"
        }
      ]
    },
